# Cursor Rules for AI-Assisted Development

## Code Standards
- Use consistent naming conventions (camelCase for variables, PascalCase for classes)
- Add type annotations/hints to all functions
- Write JSDoc/docstrings for all public APIs
- Keep functions under 50 lines when possible
- Use meaningful variable names (avoid a, b, x)

## Architecture
- Follow existing project structure
- Group related functionality together
- Minimize external dependencies
- Use composition over inheritance
- Keep modules focused and testable

## Testing
- Write tests for all new functionality
- Aim for >80% code coverage
- Use existing test framework
- Include edge case tests
- Test both happy paths and error states

## Version Control
- Make atomic, logical commits
- Use clear commit messages
- Reference issue numbers if applicable
- Don't commit local config or secrets
- Run tests before committing

## Documentation
- Update README with API changes
- Add comments for complex logic
- Include usage examples
- Document breaking changes
- Keep docs in sync with code

## Performance
- Avoid unnecessary loops and conditionals
- Cache computed values when appropriate
- Use efficient data structures
- Profile before optimizing
- Document performance considerations

## Security
- Validate all inputs
- Sanitize user data
- Use secure APIs
- Avoid hardcoding secrets
- Keep dependencies updated

## Error Handling
- Use try-catch for recoverable errors
- Log meaningful error messages
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Document expected exceptions
- Handle async errors properly

## Common Patterns
- Use async/await over .then()
- Prefer const/let over var
- Use arrow functions for callbacks
- Use template literals for strings
- Use destructuring when beneficial

## Dependency Management
- Review new dependencies before adding
- Use exact versions in production
- Keep dependencies up to date
- Remove unused dependencies
- Document dependency purpose

## Accessibility & Usability
- Ensure code is readable
- Use clear variable naming
- Avoid magic numbers
- Add configuration options
- Support both CLI and programmatic usage

---

These rules apply to all AI-assisted code generation and human review.
